 By Neil Irwin and Courtenay Brown &middot; Oct 02, 2026 
 
 
 The September jobs report came in awfully soft. We look at why — and a few silver linings — in today's newsletter.

 Plus, how the weak payrolls reading dovetails with this week's messaging from the Federal Reserve that aimed to pump the brakes on talk of another rate hike this month. Today's newsletter, edited by Jeffrey Cane and copy edited by Amy Stern, is 830 words, a 3-minute read. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: No labor market tightening here… 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Bureau of Labor Statistics; Chart: Neil Irwin/Axios Job creation was minimal in September and weaker over the summer than was thought — taking some of the apparent buoyancy out of the labor market picture.

 Why it matters: This is one of those instances where the data tells us more about what isn't happening than what is. 

 And the labor market decidedly doesn't look to be heating up — certainly not in a way that might compel more aggressive monetary tightening by the Fed. Under the hood, the data on the September job market is more mixed — and in some ways even more optimistic — than gloomy headlines this morning might suggest. By the numbers: Employers added 29,000 jobs in September, well short of the 84,000 analysts had expected. The Bureau of Labor Statistics also revised July and August numbers down by 60,000 jobs.

 Tally it up and the three-month average is 51,000 jobs added a month, well below the pace in the spring. The unemployment rate, meanwhile, ticked up to 4.2%, from 4.1%. The silver lining there is that it was for good reasons: more Americans looking for work. The labor force participation rate and the employment-to-population ratio both ticked up. Reality check: Job growth is a hard data point to parse right now because nobody is quite sure what it should be — what steady-state, full-employment payrolls growth would look like given the volatile immigration policy.

 Meanwhile, the rise in labor force participation helps assuage worries from earlier in the year that prime-age adults were skipping out of the workforce entirely. What they're saying: "Not every increase in the unemployment rate is a warning," Jason Pride of Glenmede wrote in a note, adding that "this one in particular reads as a sign of confidence rather than distress."

 "After spending most of this year at the low end of the range consistent with full employment, a tick higher in the unemployment rate matters far less than the reason behind it, and workers choosing to come off the sidelines is an encouraging signal about how they view their prospects." Zoom out: Put it together, and the overall picture that the September numbers paint is one of a labor market in balance — or in other words, one that is neither falling apart nor at much risk of overheating.

 There have been hints that the job market was reaccelerating earlier this year. That no longer looks to be the case — rather it is just rumbling along steadily in the background while other economic headlines draw attention from markets and the Fed. The bottom line: In an environment of elevated inflation, surging energy costs, rising long-term interest rates and pressure on the Fed to do something about it, labor market stability is at least one less thing to worry about.

 
 
