 2. What it means for the Fed 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Fed vice chair Philip Jefferson. Photo: Kevin Dietsch/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Even before the soft September numbers, Fed leadership was sending clear signals that they are disinclined to raise interest rates again at a meeting late this month.

 The absence of labor market tightening likely affirms that view. Catch up quick: Fed vice chair Philip Jefferson said yesterday that coming to a judgment about further rate hikes "may take more time."

 He spoke of the value of waiting to have "more data in hand" before making another move. Those comments came on the heels of New York Fed president John Williams saying earlier in the week that thanks to the September rate hike, "there is no need for urgency, and we have time to gather more information." "The accumulation of more data should provide greater clarity on the underlying trends in the economy and the associated risks to achieving our goals — and thereby the appropriate setting of monetary policy," Williams said. Between the lines: The vice chair and New York Fed chief traditionally serve as part of a leadership triumvirate (along with the Fed chairman) on monetary policy matters, so their words carry extra weight.

 While chairman Kevin Warsh had no role in selecting either Jefferson or Williams, and it's been an open question how the Fed's leadership might evolve under his chairmanship, it's hard to imagine Jefferson and Williams freelancing with such explicit near-term policy signals. Rather, this looks like a coordinated effort to pump the brakes on market expectations of a late October rate hike, a direction that is even more justifiable given the absence of labor market tightening seen in today's data. The bottom line: The Fed is more laser-focused on inflation right now than the job market, and September inflation data is still to come. But the soft job growth numbers, negative revisions, and uptick in unemployment all point to patience on rate hikes.

 
 
